    {"id": "v-la-colombe", "cat": "berth", "tier": "berth_top", "priority": 2, "badge": "MICHELIN-LISTED", "name": "La Colombe", "short": "La Colombe", "lat": 43.1288, "lng": 6.1085, "tags": ["Hyères · La Bayorre", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 17 Oct)"], "productTags": ["Provençal table — Bib Gourmand"], "why": "Pascal Bonamy cooks and Nadège runs the room, at the foot of the Maurettes on the old road to Toulon, an address they opened in 1987. He holds the Maître Restaurateur title and belongs to the Collège Culinaire de France. MICHELIN files it under traditional cuisine and names a sesame salmon tataki and turbot with black garlic jus; the menus run from 45 to 75 euros.", "address": "663 route de Toulon, La Bayorre, 83400 Hyères, France", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=La+Colombe+The+Var", "category": "maures", "verdict": "The Var's quiet counter-argument to Riviera pricing: a Michelin Bib Gourmand where the cooking is genuinely homemade and seasonal — gurnard fillet in a swimming-crab bouillon, not a harbour view. Expect a full Provençal menu (lunch from about €45, tasting menus to about €75) at value-driven money, held to the same line since 1987. If it ever reads as tourist cooking or coastal-terrace prices, this verdict is wrong.", "signature": "Filet de rouget grondin, bouillon d'étrilles et croûtons à la rouille — gurnard (rouget-grondin) fillet in a velvet swimming-crab bouillon with rouille croutons (quoted verbatim from the restaurant's own menu; seasonal, rotates).", "caveat": "Inland and roadside at the foot of the Maurettes, not portside — no sea view, and you'll want a car. It's a two-person family house (Pascal in the kitchen, Nadège in the room), so service is limited: closed Monday and Tuesday and some services vary by season, so call ahead before you go.", "person": "Pascal & Nadège Bonamy", "signal_chip": {"label": "MICHELIN", "full": "Listed in the MICHELIN Guide (traditional cuisine). Bib Gourmand cited by tourism listings and older sources (awarded 2015 per Le Var des Gastronomes) but not confirmed in any 2026 MICHELIN extract found.", "cosign": "MICHELIN Guide; Gilles Pudlowski (July 2024)"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-17", "hook": "Pascal and Nadège Bonamy's Provençal table on the Toulon road out of Hyères, open since 1987.", "dishes": [{"name": "Filet de rouget grondin, bouillon d'étrilles", "note": "Gurnard in swimming-crab broth with rouille croutons, from the house menu (seasonal)", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Sesame salmon tataki", "note": "Named in the MICHELIN write-up", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Turbot fillet, black garlic jus", "note": "Also named by MICHELIN", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Year-round (tourism listing updated 30 Apr 2026); open now · Wed-Sun; closed Monday and Tuesday (tourism listing 2026, MICHELIN)", "phone": "+33 4 94 35 35 16", "price_range": "Menus and carte EUR 45-75 (tourism listing 2026, seen Oct 2026)", "reservation": "Book; small family house", "neighborhood": "Hyères · La Bayorre", "charter": {"price": "Menus and carte EUR 45-75 (tourism listing 2026, seen Oct 2026)", "book": "Book; small family house", "warn": "Inland and roadside at the foot of the Maurettes, not portside — no sea view, and you'll want a car. It's a two-person family house (Pascal in the kitchen, Nadège in the room), so service is limited: closed Monday and Tuesday and some services vary by season, so call ahead before you go.", "fit": "Pascal and Nadège Bonamy's Provençal table on the Toulon road out of Hyères, open since 1987."}},
    {"id": "v-chateau-de-pibarnon", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 12, "badge": "G&M 93/100", "name": "Château de Pibarnon", "short": "Château de Pibarnon", "lat": 43.175582, "lng": 5.750889, "tags": ["La Cadière-d'Azur · Bandol", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["AOC Bandol domaine · Mourvèdre"], "why": "Pibarnon farms about 52 hectares organically on terraces cut into a natural bowl under the Colline du Télégraphe, around 300 metres up, looking down to the bay of La Ciotat. The soil is blue marl, rare in Bandol. The red is mostly Mourvèdre with a little Grenache: the Guide Hachette 2025 gave the 2021 two stars and counted 99,000 bottles of it, with a drinking window of 2027 to 2034.", "address": "410 Chemin de la Croix des Signaux, 83740 La Cadière-d'Azur, Var", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Ch%C3%A2teau+de+Pibarnon+The+Var", "category": "domaines", "verdict": "Bandol's highest vineyard — the Mourvèdre climbs to 300m on the Colline du Télégraphe, planted on rare blue marl, and the flagship red is built to age a decade or more before it opens. Come for the amphitheatre-and-sea view; cellar the wine for later.", "signature": "The Bandol rouge — roughly 90% Mourvèdre / 10% Grenache, grown on the estate's blue marl (marnes bleues): a classic vin de garde built for long ageing.", "caveat": "Tasting and sales at the property (Guide Hachette); no 2026 cellar-door hours found, so call ahead. The earlier record's Mon-Sat hours are not re-confirmed. Young vintages of the red are tight and want years.", "person": "Éric de Saint-Victor", "signal_chip": {"label": "G&M 93/100", "full": "Gault&Millau rates the Château de Pibarnon 2020 Bandol rouge 93/100 (2019: 91/100); Guide Hachette 2025 gives the 2021 rouge two stars", "cosign": "Gault&Millau; Guide Hachette des Vins 2025"}, "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-17", "hook": "Bandol's high amphitheatre of Mourvèdre on blue marl above La Cadière, owned by Éric de Saint-Victor.", "dishes": [{"name": "Bandol rouge", "note": "About 90% Mourvèdre; 2020 rated 93/100 and 2019 91/100 by Gault&Millau; built to age", "kind": "drink"}, {"name": "Les Restanques de Pibarnon rouge", "note": "Younger-vine red, 70% Mourvèdre, for earlier drinking", "kind": "drink"}, {"name": "Bandol rosé", "note": "The estate rosé, the easier bottle for lunch", "kind": "drink"}, {"name": "Bandol blanc", "note": "The estate white, sold by Swiss merchant Cavesa (2021 vintage)", "kind": "drink"}], "phone": "+33 4 94 90 12 73", "neighborhood": "La Cadière-d'Azur · Bandol"},
    {"id": "v-domaine-de-terrebrune", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 13, "badge": "G&M 90/100", "name": "Domaine de Terrebrune", "short": "Domaine de Terrebrune", "lat": 43.136, "lng": 5.832, "tags": ["Ollioules · Bandol", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 17 Oct)"], "productTags": ["Bandol AOC · organic & biodynamic Mourvèdre estate"], "why": "Georges Delille, a Paris sommelier, bought the land at Ollioules in 1963 and dug the ageing cellars into the Triassic rock of the hillside; his son Reynald bottled the first Terrebrune in 1980. Today about 35 hectares are farmed organically, with Mourvèdre the main grape, and Jean d'Arthuys has co-owned the estate since 2020. A guided cellar visit and tasting costs 15 euros, deducted from what you buy.", "address": "724 chemin de la Tourelle, 83190 Ollioules", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Domaine+de+Terrebrune+The+Var", "category": "domaines", "verdict": "A western-Var Bandol reference: certified-organic (and Biodyvin biodynamic) Mourvèdre grown on sea-facing terraces of brown Trias clay, over the blue, fissured Triassic limestone that lends the wines their minerality, and aged in galleries cut into the hillside rock. Falsifiable proof it punches above a roadside cellar-door — Gault&Millau scores the 2020 red 90/100, and the ~€15 guided cave-tour-plus-tasting is deducted from whatever you buy.", "signature": "Bandol Rouge \"Terroir du Trias\" — roughly 85% Mourvèdre with Grenache and Cinsault (about 85/10/5), raised around 18 months in large old oak foudres of 40–60 hl; the domaine's structured, mineral flagship.", "caveat": "Strictly by appointment (book via terrebrune.fr), closed Sundays and public holidays; the ~€15 is per person and only redeemable against a purchase. It is a working family domaine, not a polished tasting-room. I can verify it is a certified-organic, Biodyvin Bandol benchmark — I cannot independently confirm the superlative \"one of the western Var's greatest,\" so treat that as enthusiasm, not fact.", "person": "Reynald Delille — winemaker and co-owner; joined founder-father Georges Delille (an ex-Paris sommelier who bought the land in 1963) and bottled the first Terrebrune vintage in 1980. Now co-owned with Jean d'Arthuys (joined 2020).", "signal_chip": {"label": "G&M 90/100", "full": "Gault&Millau rates the 2020 Bandol rouge 90/100; Guide Hachette 2026 reviews the 2024 Bandol blanc", "cosign": "Gault&Millau; Guide Hachette des Vins 2026"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-17", "hook": "Organic Bandol estate in Ollioules, Mourvèdre on Triassic soils, aged in galleries cut into the rock.", "dishes": [{"name": "Bandol rouge Terroir du Trias", "note": "About 85% Mourvèdre, 18 months in large foudres; the 2020 scored 90/100 with Gault&Millau", "kind": "drink"}, {"name": "Bandol blanc", "note": "The 2024 vintage is reviewed in the Guide Hachette 2026", "kind": "drink"}], "phone": "+33 4 94 74 01 30", "web": "https://www.terrebrune.fr", "price_range": "Guided cellar visit and tasting EUR 15 per person, deducted from purchases (regional tourism listing, seen Oct 2026)", "reservation": "Guided visits by appointment via the estate site", "hours": "Cellar door and shop Monday to Saturday (online guide; times not given)", "neighborhood": "Ollioules · Bandol"},
    {"id": "v-la-commanderie-de-peyrassol", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 14, "badge": "MICHELIN 1 STAR", "name": "La Commanderie de Peyrassol", "short": "La Commanderie de Peyrassol", "lat": 43.3797, "lng": 6.2637, "tags": ["Flassans-sur-Issole", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 17 Oct)"], "productTags": ["Côtes de Provence wine estate with sculpture park and Michelin-starred table"], "why": "Peyrassol was a commandery of the Knights Templar from the 13th century. Philippe Austruy bought it in 2001 and has set some sixty monumental sculptures among the vines. Its restaurant, Chez Jeannette, took a MICHELIN star in March 2025 under Benjamin Le Balch, who came from L'Aparté in Geneva, and kept it in 2026; his menu follows the estate kitchen garden rather than a fixed carte.", "address": "1204 chemin de la Commanderie de Peyrassol, RN7, 83340 Flassans-sur-Issole, Var, France", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=La+Commanderie+de+Peyrassol+The+Var", "category": "domaines", "verdict": "The rare inland detour worth leaving the coast for: a 13th-century Knights Templar commanderie where the same day buys a cellar tasting, a walk through a major open-air sculpture park of some sixty monumental works, and lunch at Chez Jeannette — which holds one Michelin star (Michelin Guide, awarded 31 March 2025). Falsifiable: if Chez Jeannette loses or lacks the star, this card is wrong.", "signature": "The estate's calling card is the Cuvée des Commandeurs rosé — a Grenache-led AOP Côtes de Provence drawn from its best parcels (2023 vintage rated 90 points, Vinous). At table, chef Benjamin Le Balch cooks vegetable-first, no fixed signature: an emblematic summer plate is courgette worked burnt, raw and cooked with black lemon, basil, olive oil and bergamot.", "caveat": "Not a beach stop and not casual. The domaine sits in the Maures foothills off the RN7, roughly 25 minutes inland from the Var coast. Chez Jeannette is a reservation-only splurge — lunch Thursday to Monday, dinner on select evenings (days shift with the season, so confirm when you book) — with no fixed signature dishes; the menu turns with the kitchen garden. Sculpture-park entry and tastings are ticketed and best booked ahead, not a walk-in.", "person": "Benjamin Le Balch — chef of Chez Jeannette (one Michelin star, 2025); estate owned by Philippe Austruy since 2001.", "signal_chip": {"label": "1 star 2026", "full": "Chez Jeannette, chef Benjamin Le Balch: one MICHELIN star, first awarded March 2025 and retained in the MICHELIN Guide France 2026 (per the estate and Do It In Paris); Gault&Millau 2 toques 2024", "cosign": "MICHELIN Guide 2026"}, "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-17", "hook": "Templar-era wine estate off the RN7 with a sculpture park and Chez Jeannette, one MICHELIN star.", "dishes": [{"name": "Cuvée des Commandeurs rosé", "note": "Grenache-led Côtes de Provence from the best parcels; 2023 rated 90 by Vinous", "kind": "drink"}, {"name": "Courgette, burnt, raw and cooked", "note": "With black lemon, basil, olive oil and bergamot; a summer plate from Le Balch's garden menu", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Chez Jeannette open 14 Feb to 21 Dec 2026 (Coeur du Var tourism office, updated 17 Jun 2026); open now; winter closure from 22 Dec, reopening expected early Feb 2027 (not announced) · Closed Tuesday and Wednesday (tourism office 2026, MICHELIN)", "phone": "+33 4 94 69 71 02", "web": "https://www.peyrassol.com", "reservation": "Book Chez Jeannette; tastings and the sculpture park are ticketed", "neighborhood": "Flassans-sur-Issole", "charter": {"book": "Book Chez Jeannette; tastings and the sculpture park are ticketed", "warn": "Not a beach stop and not casual. The domaine sits in the Maures foothills off the RN7, roughly 25 minutes inland from the Var coast. Chez Jeannette is a reservation-only splurge — lunch Thursday to Monday, dinner on select evenings (days shift with the season, so confirm when you book) — with no fixed signature dishes; the menu turns with the kitchen garden. Sculpture-park entry and tastings are ticketed and best booked ahead, not a walk-in.", "fit": "Templar-era wine estate off the RN7 with a sculpture park and Chez Jeannette, one MICHELIN star."}},
    {"id": "v-la-cade-a-dede", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 22, "badge": "SINCE 1989", "name": "La Cade à Dédé", "short": "La Cade à Dédé", "neighborhood": "Toulon · Cours Lafayette", "tags": ["Toulon · Cours Lafayette", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["cade", "socca", "chickpea"], "why": "Cade is the chickpea-flour galette of Toulon, thicker and softer than the socca of Nice, and tied by local tradition to Genoese workers who settled near the arsenal. At La Cade à Dédé the oven is a wood-fired one built onto a trailer, parked at the Cours Lafayette market; a Toulon food-tour blog names the cook as Alain, gives 2.50 euros a portion and says you can pick your piece from the sheet.", "address": "Cours Lafayette (about no. 20), 83000 Toulon", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=La+Cade+a+Dede+Var+Provence", "category": "street", "verdict": "Two mobile carts since 1989, cooking the toulonnais answer to Nicois socca: cade, thicker, tender underneath, chickpea-flour, wood-fired. The one thing to learn here: Nice's socca is thin and crisp; Toulon's cade is thick and soft, brought by 19th-century Genoese workers who settled around the arsenal. Money eats by definition. Falsifiable: open daily except Monday, roughly 8:00 to 12:45.", "signature": "A hot slab of cade, plain first, then the anchovy or olive version, eaten standing and folded in paper before it steams itself soft.", "person": "\"Dede\"", "caveat": "Only one source (a food-tour company's blog) found; the second cart at Le Mourillon in the earlier record was not confirmed. Morning only.", "hook": "Cade cart with a wood-fired oven on a trailer, at the Cours Lafayette market in Toulon.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Since 1989", "full": "Cade cart at the Cours Lafayette market, running since 1989 per the earlier record and tourism copy; cook named Alain by Do Eat Better", "cosign": "Do Eat Better Experience; Provence Méditerranée tourism"}, "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-17", "dishes": [{"name": "Cade", "note": "A portion cut from the sheet, peppered, eaten hot in paper; about EUR 2.50 per the blog", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Edge piece", "note": "Ask for the border of the tray for a crisper bite", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Year-round with the market (per blog) · Tuesday to Sunday mornings until 12:30 (Do Eat Better blog, undated); closed Monday", "price_range": "About EUR 2.50 a portion (Do Eat Better blog, seen Oct 2026)", "hot_this_month": "Autumn mornings at the Cours Lafayette and Le Mourillon carts, every day but Monday, roughly 8:00–12:45: a hot cade in paper before the market (checked 17 Oct 2026)."},
    {"id": "v-marche-du-cours-lafayette", "cat": "berth", "tier": "berth_top", "priority": 3, "badge": "MORNING MARKET", "name": "Marché du Cours Lafayette", "short": "Marché du Cours Lafayette", "lat": 43.1245, "lng": 5.9345, "tags": ["Toulon · Cours Lafayette", "Confirmed trading 2026 (checked 17 Oct)"], "productTags": ["Provençal street market"], "why": "Every morning but Monday the Cours Lafayette, on the edge of Toulon's old town, is lined for about a kilometre with stalls of Provençal growers, fishmongers, cheese and charcuterie, spilling into Place Louis Blanc. Gilbert Bécaud, born in Toulon in 1927, sang it in 1957 in Les marchés de Provence. In the afternoon a separate non-food market takes the same pavement, from 2:30 to 6:30.", "address": "Cours Lafayette (extending to cours Paul Lendrin and Place Louis Blanc), 83000 Toulon, Var, France", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=March%C3%A9+du+Cours+Lafayette+The+Var", "category": "street", "verdict": "Go Tuesday to Sunday, roughly 7.30am to 12.30pm, for the food market — a genuine working produce, fish and charcuterie market that unspools a full kilometre down the micocoulier-lined cours and spills into Place Louis Blanc and cours Paul Lendrin. Come in the afternoon and only the non-food forain stalls remain (about 2.30 to 6.30pm); come on Monday and it is dark. This is falsifiable: the days and morning hours are the test.", "signature": "La cade toulonnaise — a chickpea-flour galette baked over a wood fire, dusted with pepper and eaten hot straight from a paper cornet, from the Cade à Dédé carts (running since 1989) at the Place Louis Blanc end of the market.", "caveat": "This earns no Michelin, Gault&Millau, Fooding or Condé Nast-grade signal — none exists for the market itself; the \"most beautiful market\" laurel is TF1's \"Votre plus beau marché\" public vote (a 2021 regional title) plus tourism-board copy, i.e. a popularity contest, not a curated endorsement. In July and August it turns shoulder-to-shoulder and tilts toward tourist stroll rather than everyday shop. Food stalls pack up by about 12.30pm and it is closed Mondays.", "person": "Gilbert Bécaud", "status": "confirmed", "statusChecked": "2026-10-17", "hook": "Toulon's morning food market along the Cours Lafayette, Tuesday to Sunday, 7:30 to 12:30.", "dishes": [{"name": "Cade from La Cade à Dédé", "note": "Hot chickpea galette from the wood-fired cart", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Provençal fruit and vegetables", "note": "From the growers' stalls", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Fish of the morning", "note": "From the market's fishmongers", "kind": "dish"}, {"name": "Cheese and charcuterie", "note": "From the market's cheese and pork stalls", "kind": "dish"}], "hours": "Year-round · Food market Tue-Sun 07:30-12:30 (Le Lavandou/Var tourism listing for 1 Jan-31 Dec 2026); non-food forain market Tue-Sun 14:30-18:30; closed Monday (the regional listing's Monday note is ambiguous)", "neighborhood": "Toulon · Cours Lafayette", "signal_chip": {"label": "Tue-Sun AM", "full": "Food market open Tuesday to Sunday 7:30-12:30 all 2026; TF1 'Votre plus beau marché' regional title 2021", "cosign": "Var tourism offices"}, "hot_this_month": "The off-season is when Toulon has its market to itself: Tuesday to Sunday mornings, roughly 7:30–12:30, with the cade carts at Place Louis Blanc (checked 17 Oct 2026)."},
    {"id": "v-confiserie-azureenne", "cat": "shop", "tier": "plenty", "priority": 16, "badge": "SINCE 1948", "name": "Confiserie Azuréenne", "short": "Confiserie Azuréenne", "neighborhood": "Collobrières", "tags": ["Collobrières", "Not confirmed for 2026 — call ahead"], "productTags": ["marron-glace", "chestnut", "confiserie"], "why": "The Maures chestnut has been candied in Collobrières since 1948 at this house on the Boulevard Koenig, which makes marrons glacés, chestnut cream, chestnut ice cream and candied fruit. Alongside the shop, the Musée de la fabrique du marron glacé shows the tools and machines once used to sort, peel and candy the nuts, and entry is free. The village's chestnut festival runs on three Sundays, 11 to 25 October 2026.", "address": "Boulevard Kœnig, 83610 Collobrières", "maps": "https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Confiserie+Azureenne+Var+Provence", "category": "street", "verdict": "Not a table but a workshop that has candied chestnuts here since 1948, with a free little museum of the old sorting machines and copper pans out back. The marron glace is the genuine article; the creme de marrons is what you actually carry home. Falsifiable: the marron-glace museum is free to enter.", "signature": "A jar of creme de marrons plus a few loose marrons glaces: the glace for the ceremony, the cream because it turns any yoghurt or crepe into Collobrieres.", "person": "Jean-Louis Corsiglia (owner)", "caveat": "Not a restaurant. No 2026-dated item specific to the house found; the chestnut festival (Sundays 11, 18, 25 Oct 2026) is running now and the village is busy on those days.", "hook": "Collobrières chestnut confectioner since 1948, with a free museum of the old candying machines.", "signal_chip": {"label": "Since 1948", "full": "Candying Maures chestnuts since 1948; free Musée de la fabrique du marron glacé", "cosign": "Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur Tourisme; Le Lavandou tourism office"}, "status": "unverified", "statusChecked": "2026-10-17", "dishes": [{"name": "Marrons glacés", "note": "The candied whole chestnut, the house's founding product", "kind": "sweet"}, {"name": "Crème de marrons", "note": "Chestnut spread, the jar most people carry home", "kind": "sweet"}, {"name": "Chestnut ice cream", "note": "Made by the house", "kind": "sweet"}, {"name": "Candied fruit", "note": "Fruits confits from the same kitchen", "kind": "sweet"}], "hot_this_month": "Chestnut month: the Fêtes de la Châtaigne fill Collobrières on Sundays 18 and 25 October; buy the crème de marrons and see the free marron-glacé museum (checked 17 Oct 2026)."}
