  const GEMS = [{"id": "gem-tropezienne", "pattern": "tropézienne", "tag": "Var gem · born here", "name": "A cake named by a film star", "story": "Alexandre Micka, a Polish pastry-maker, settled in Saint-Tropez in 1945 with a family recipe for a sugar-crusted brioche filled with cream. In 1955 he was catering the shoot of Et Dieu… créa la femme and served it to Brigitte Bardot, who suggested he call it the tart of Saint-Tropez. He kept the idea and registered the name Tarte Tropézienne in 1973.", "where": "La Tarte Tropézienne, the house Micka founded, in Saint-Tropez."}, {"id": "gem-chichi", "pattern": "chichi", "tag": "Var gem · born here", "name": "The fritter with two birthplaces", "story": "Toulon credits Alexis Guglielmi, who sold his orange-blossom chickpea fritters on the Cours Lafayette from 1907 and claimed to have invented them; the family stall closed in 2022. The claim is disputed: the treat turns up in Provençal writing decades earlier, and Marseille’s L’Estaque calls it its own.", "where": "The kiosks by the sea and at the fairgrounds of Toulon."}, {"id": "gem-cade", "pattern": "the cade", "tag": "Var gem · born here", "name": "Soldiers, Genoese and a word for hot", "story": "Toulon’s chickpea galette has two origin stories and no proof for either. One says Napoleonic soldiers brought it and the Italian caldo, hot, became cade; the other, told at the carts, credits the Genoese workers who settled round the arsenal in the nineteenth century. Both agree it is Ligurian at heart, a cousin of the farinata of Genoa.", "where": "La Cade à Dédé’s carts at the Place Louis Blanc end of the Cours Lafayette."}];
