  const CATEGORIES = [{"key": "summit", "label": "The summit — three stars and two", "lead": "Donckele’s sauces on the Bouillabaisse beach, a three-star in the Bandol hills, and Canino’s seawater-poached red mullet at Ramatuelle.", "story": {"title": "Three stars, two stars", "story": "The Var's top tables sit inside hotels, and two of the three keep summer hours. La Vague d'Or, on the Bouillabaisse beach at Cheval Blanc St-Tropez, has held three stars since 2013 under Arnaud Donckele and in 2026 opened only from 7 May to 10 October. La Table du Castellet, beside the Circuit Paul Ricard, went from no stars to three in March 2024, when Fabien Ferré, Christophe Bacquié's former second, was 35; it is the one that stays open into the autumn, until early December. La Voile, at La Réserve Ramatuelle, has held two stars since 2020 for Éric Canino, a Michel Guérard pupil who cooks with olive oil instead of butter and serves an all-vegetable menu.", "where": "La Vague d'Or, Saint-Tropez; La Table du Castellet, Le Castellet; La Voile, Ramatuelle"}}, {"key": "seastar", "label": "One star by the sea", "lead": "A vegetable-forward room on the Salins road, a terrace on the red rocks of the Esterel, and Toulon’s own star by the opera.", "story": {"title": "One star along the coast", "story": "Along the Var coast, the one-star tables are vegetable-led rooms that cook with the local catch. Colette, at Hôtel Sezz on the route des Salins, has had a star since 2021 under Philippe Colinet; one of its two dinner menus is entirely plant-based, and the room closes in early October. Récif, on the roof of Les Roches Rouges at Boulouris, kept the star José Bailly won in 2019 after Alexandre Baule took over in May 2025; he cooks here in summer and in Megève in winter. Shanael, by the Toulon opera, gave the city its first starred table since 2001 in March 2026, and as a city restaurant it is the only one of the three that opens all year.", "where": "Colette, Saint-Tropez; Récif, Saint-Raphaël; Shanael, Toulon"}}, {"key": "inland", "label": "The hinterland houses — the truffle, the garden, the abbey", "lead": "The truffle house of Lorgues, a golf resort’s Provençal kitchen at Tourrettes, and Ducasse’s abbey inn at La Celle.", "story": {"title": "Truffle, garden and abbey", "story": "Away from the beaches, the Var's serious tables cook from the hinterland's own larder. At Chez Bruno, in open country outside Lorgues, every course is truffle; Clément Bruno, who built the house, died in January 2025, and his sons Benjamin and Samuel run it. Le Faventia, at Terre Blanche above Fayence, has Quentin André cooking Tourves vegetables, Saint-Raphaël fish and Vidauban poultry, from late spring to 26 October. The Hostellerie de l'Abbaye de la Celle, Alain Ducasse's inn in Provence Verte, lost its star in 2024; Nicolas Pierantoni, born nearby, still cooks mostly from its kitchen garden and keeps two Gault&Millau toques.", "where": "Chez Bruno, Lorgues; Le Faventia, Tourrettes; Hostellerie de l'Abbaye de la Celle, La Celle"}}, {"key": "sand", "label": "On the sand — Pampelonne and the islands", "lead": "The family beach of 1955 at Pampelonne, and the two tables of Porquerolles you reach on foot or by the hotel’s shuttle.", "story": {"title": "Lunch on the beach", "story": "These are lunch tables on the sand, open from spring to autumn, where the menu is grilled fish and whatever the garden gives. Club 55 began on Pampelonne in 1955 by feeding Roger Vadim's film crew; the de Colmont family still runs it, the crudités come from their farm, and in 2026 it is open until 1 November. On Porquerolles, where visitors cannot bring cars, the Le Ber family has grilled wild fish above the Plage d'Argent since 1957, from April to 1 October. At the island's western tip, La Pinède at the Mas du Langoustier serves whole langouste to share and is reached by the hotel's shuttle; its 2026 season ended in late September.", "where": "Club 55, Ramatuelle; La Plage d'Argent, Porquerolles; La Pinède at Le Mas du Langoustier, Porquerolles"}}, {"key": "houses", "label": "The old houses — dated", "lead": "The fishermen’s inn of the old quarter, the roadside auberge of 1962 in the Maures, and the post house of Barjols with its Friday aïoli.", "story": {"title": "Three old houses, three kitchens", "story": "The Var's old houses are inns and hotels that outlived the trade they were built for. Hôtel La Ponche began as a 1930s fishermen's bar on the Saint-Tropez cove of the same name; MICHELIN now lists its restaurant without a star, with Simon Pinault cooking the day's catch. The Auberge de la Môle has served Southwest France, foie gras and frogs' legs, to the coast since 1962, cash only, with its 2026 season listed as ending on 11 October. Le Pont d'Or in Barjols was a coaching inn and has been kept by one family for four generations; it opens from mid-January to the end of November and is known locally for a Friday aïoli.", "where": "Hôtel La Ponche, Saint-Tropez; Auberge de la Môle, La Môle; Le Pont d'Or, Barjols"}}, {"key": "hautvar", "label": "The Haut-Var village tables", "lead": "The daube of Ampus, the truffle bistro of Aups, and a committed small kitchen in Cotignac.", "story": {"title": "Village tables of the Haut-Var", "story": "Up in the Haut-Var the hill villages keep few tables, and each one counts for the village. In Ampus, at about 600 metres above the Nartuby gorges, the Auberge des Braconniers joined the Bistrot de Pays network in 2021, a label that asks for local producers; it serves daube next to wood-fired pizza. In Aups, which holds a black truffle market every January, Valentine Lorcher and Adrien Antelme opened Solea in 2024; Gault&Millau scores it 12.5/20. In Cotignac, Picotte, the southern branch of a Paris bistro opened in 2020, cooks southern regional dishes on rue Saint-Jean.", "where": "L'Auberge des Braconniers, Ampus; Solea, Aups; Picotte Provence, Cotignac"}}, {"key": "maures", "label": "The Maures and the palm coast", "lead": "The village table of Collobrières, a medieval lane in Bormes, and the Bib Gourmand house below the Maurettes.", "story": {"title": "Forest villages and the Hyères road", "story": "The Massif des Maures is chestnut and cork-oak country behind the coast, and its tables cook from it. In Collobrières, whose chestnut festival runs on three October Sundays in 2026, La Petite Fontaine serves daube, petits farcis and supions on a 34-euro menu. In the old village of Bormes, Lou Portaou is built into the vaulted passage of the ruelle des Sarrasins, held by Philippe Cavatore since 1986; the tourism office gives its 2026 season as February to August. On the Toulon road out of Hyères, Pascal and Nadège Bonamy have run La Colombe since 1987, year-round, with menus from 45 to 75 euros.", "where": "La Petite Fontaine, Collobrières; Lou Portaou, Bormes-les-Mimosas; La Colombe, Hyères"}}, {"key": "domaines", "label": "The cellar doors — Bandol and the Côtes", "lead": "Mourvèdre on the amphitheatre of La Cadière, the organic estate of Ollioules, and the Templars’ commandery on the RN7.", "story": {"title": "Mourvèdre, marl and a Templar estate", "story": "Bandol's reds are built on Mourvèdre, a late-ripening grape that needs the sun and the sea air of this coast, and the Côtes de Provence inland make mostly rosé. Château de Pibarnon farms about 52 hectares organically on blue marl in a bowl of terraces above La Cadière; Gault&Millau gives its 2020 red 93/100. Domaine de Terrebrune in Ollioules ages its wine in galleries Georges Delille cut into Triassic rock after 1963, and offers a 15-euro visit. La Commanderie de Peyrassol, a Templar commandery from the 13th century, adds a sculpture park and Chez Jeannette, which kept its MICHELIN star in 2026.", "where": "Château de Pibarnon, La Cadière-d'Azur; Domaine de Terrebrune, Ollioules; La Commanderie de Peyrassol, Flassans-sur-Issole"}}, {"key": "street", "label": "La Rue — the cade, the market street, the chestnut", "lead": "The cade carts of 1989, the kilometre of the Cours Lafayette, and the marrons glacés of Collobrières.", "story": {"title": "Cade, market stalls, candied chestnuts", "story": "The Var's street food is cheap, made in the morning and sold standing up. Cade, a chickpea-flour galette thicker and softer than the socca of Nice, is Toulon's own, and La Cade à Dédé bakes it in a wood-fired oven on a trailer at the Cours Lafayette for about 2.50 euros a portion. The Cours Lafayette market itself, which Gilbert Bécaud sang in 1957, runs Tuesday to Sunday from 7:30 to 12:30 all year. Inland in Collobrières, the Confiserie Azuréenne has candied Maures chestnuts since 1948 and keeps a free museum of its old machines.", "where": "La Cade à Dédé, Toulon; Marché du Cours Lafayette, Toulon; Confiserie Azuréenne, Collobrières"}}];
